  [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row's own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth's printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.  QUESTION (added 2026-09-08, after codex settled the disposition and left the hardware claim open): The DISPOSITION is now settled by codex against #447's actual implementation: decline the out-of-set write and PRESERVE the previous value (the queue's option B), with the store moved BELOW the guard -- because today the store runs FIRST (dev_lca.c:233 vs the guard at :236), so merely deleting the exit(1) leaves the bad value STORED and guest-readable.  What is NOT settled, and is this row's research question: **what does the real 21066 LCA do with an out-of-set value in these six registers?**  We have NO LCA datasheet on this machine -- `_scratchpad/*.pdf` holds none -- and the only primary source is the OpenBSD 2.2 driver (`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c`), which shows what a GUEST writes and not what the CHIP accepts.  So: (a) is decline-and-preserve defensible as MODELLING, or is it merely the safest thing to do in ignorance -- and should the round SAY the latter rather than imply the former?  (b) The guard's accepted sets are themselves unverified: W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 REJECT their own reset value of 0, and the header names 0 as IOC_W_MASK_1M, which suggests 0 is a legal encoding the whitelist omits.  **Is the correct round actually to widen or delete the whitelists rather than to change what happens when they fail?**
